00:02:50.520 --> 00:02:52.380
For those of you who may not be familiar

00:02:52.380 --> 00:02:56.700
with Bangkok at Thailand and
the refugee situations there,

00:02:56.700 --> 00:02:59.040
please allow me to provide some context

00:02:59.040 --> 00:03:01.680
and factual information about the cities

00:03:01.680 --> 00:03:04.200
and countries which can
help lay foundations

00:03:04.200 --> 00:03:06.000
for my lectures today.

00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:10.230
First, Thailand is a country
located in south Asia

00:03:10.230 --> 00:03:12.810
with Bangkok being its capital.

00:03:12.810 --> 00:03:15.780
Thailand has approximately
70 million people,

00:03:15.780 --> 00:03:20.780
around 15% of whom or around
10.5 million live in Bangkok.

00:03:21.540 --> 00:03:24.690
And if we include in adjacent provinces

00:03:24.690 --> 00:03:27.360
which form the Bangkok metropolitan areas,

00:03:27.360 --> 00:03:29.100
the entire population will increase

00:03:29.100 --> 00:03:33.570
to approximately 15 to 16 million people.

00:03:33.570 --> 00:03:35.520
Second among millions of people

00:03:35.520 --> 00:03:38.430
living in the Bangkok metropolitan areas,

00:03:38.430 --> 00:03:43.230
are thousands of urban refugees
from at least 44 countries.

00:03:43.230 --> 00:03:46.740
The official data from the
UNHCR office in Bangkok

00:03:46.740 --> 00:03:49.650
suggests that there are
five thousands of them.

00:03:49.650 --> 00:03:52.590
However, based on the
observations of both CSO

00:03:52.590 --> 00:03:54.510
and also my own research team,

00:03:54.510 --> 00:03:57.180
we think that the actual
number of refugees

00:03:57.180 --> 00:04:00.630
should be between 5,000 and 20,000 people.

00:04:00.630 --> 00:04:02.310
The major countries of origins

00:04:02.310 --> 00:04:04.620
for urban refugees in Bangkok,

00:04:04.620 --> 00:04:08.700
including Pakistan, Vietnam,
Somalia, Afghanistan,

00:04:08.700 --> 00:04:11.493
Iraq, Sri Lanka, and also Palestine.

00:04:12.450 --> 00:04:16.230
These groups of urban
refugees that I will discuss

00:04:16.230 --> 00:04:18.060
are just these groups of people.

00:04:18.060 --> 00:04:21.240
My talk will not include the
new arrivals from Myanmar,

00:04:21.240 --> 00:04:24.330
whose situation is more
complicated and complex,

00:04:24.330 --> 00:04:27.484
but please feel free to ask
questions related to them

00:04:27.484 --> 00:04:28.803
during the Q&amp;A later.

00:04:29.880 --> 00:04:32.430
Third, Thailand is one of those countries

00:04:32.430 --> 00:04:37.290
in south and southeast Asia,
which are non-signatory states

00:04:37.290 --> 00:04:38.880
during 1951 conventions

00:04:38.880 --> 00:04:42.900
relating to the status of
refugees and its 1967 protocol.

00:04:42.900 --> 00:04:46.230
However, it has a long
history of refugee management.

00:04:46.230 --> 00:04:47.940
Thailand also allows UNHCR

00:04:47.940 --> 00:04:51.053
to conduct refugees status
determination of ISD,

00:04:51.053 --> 00:04:52.995
but the irony is that the Thai government

00:04:52.995 --> 00:04:57.000
does not recognize the UNHCR
court or the refugee status.

00:04:57.000 --> 00:04:59.640
And just before the
COVID-19 pandemic started,

00:04:59.640 --> 00:05:02.970
Thailand enacted the first refugee law

00:05:02.970 --> 00:05:04.170
to determine the status

00:05:04.170 --> 00:05:07.350
of those who generally need protections.

00:05:07.350 --> 00:05:09.120
The mechanisms has become known

00:05:09.120 --> 00:05:12.844
as the national screening
mechanisms or NSM.

00:05:12.844 --> 00:05:14.850
The mechanism has not yet been enforced

00:05:14.850 --> 00:05:18.420
since the eligibility criteria

00:05:18.420 --> 00:05:20.220
that I was part of drafting committee

00:05:20.220 --> 00:05:23.700
just got approved by
the cabinet last week.

00:05:23.700 --> 00:05:26.160
The fact that Thailand
is a non-signatory state

00:05:26.160 --> 00:05:28.650
and the NSM is not yet operating,

00:05:28.650 --> 00:05:31.560
refugees continue to be illegal migrants.

00:05:31.560 --> 00:05:33.960
The terms that the Thai
Immigration Bureau use

00:05:33.960 --> 00:05:36.210
to refer to these groups of people.

00:05:36.210 --> 00:05:40.110
Officially refugees are subjected
to arrests and detention,

00:05:40.110 --> 00:05:42.903
and just on the day I arrived in the US,

00:05:44.010 --> 00:05:47.370
12 of them from the Khmer
Krom community were arrested

00:05:47.370 --> 00:05:50.550
and detained in the
Immigration Detention Center.

00:05:50.550 --> 00:05:53.400
Thus, as several scholars
of refugees in Thailand

00:05:53.400 --> 00:05:55.350
have criticized and illustrated,

00:05:55.350 --> 00:05:59.040
it is not an situation to say
that urban refugees in Bangkok

00:05:59.040 --> 00:06:00.630
continue to live in fear

00:06:00.630 --> 00:06:03.603
and also in a very hostile environment.

00:06:04.890 --> 00:06:07.950
My lectures today will
highlight apparel situations

00:06:07.950 --> 00:06:10.230
in which refugee asylums in Bangkok

00:06:10.230 --> 00:06:12.240
continues to be possible,

00:06:12.240 --> 00:06:15.300
despite what I describe as fear

00:06:15.300 --> 00:06:17.730
or hostile environment just now.

00:06:17.730 --> 00:06:18.563
More importantly,

00:06:18.563 --> 00:06:21.180
urban refugees continue
to arrive in Bangkok

00:06:21.180 --> 00:06:23.280
regardless of the
difficulties they may face

00:06:23.280 --> 00:06:25.140
or endure in the cities.

00:06:25.140 --> 00:06:27.890
And let me start my lectures
today with some anecdotes.

00:06:29.520 --> 00:06:30.920
Here's how the story begins.

00:06:31.770 --> 00:06:36.390
So while conducting field work
in Bangkok in December, 2018,

00:06:36.390 --> 00:06:40.320
I had an opportunity to meet
Junior among refugees team

00:06:40.320 --> 00:06:41.580
to a local lawyer

00:06:41.580 --> 00:06:44.180
at the church near Don
Mueang International Airport.

00:06:45.030 --> 00:06:46.950
At the time of our encounters,

00:06:46.950 --> 00:06:49.680
the lawyer did not inform
me who Junior really was,

00:06:49.680 --> 00:06:52.563
apart from saying that he
was a community volunteer.

00:06:53.400 --> 00:06:55.470
As teenagers walked toward us,

00:06:55.470 --> 00:06:57.420
Junior greeted me with a why?

00:06:57.420 --> 00:06:59.913
Xxx gestures and
introduced himself in Thai.

00:07:00.750 --> 00:07:05.313
Junior greeted me and say, Thai.

00:07:06.720 --> 00:07:09.240
His Thai was flawless.

00:07:09.240 --> 00:07:10.470
And I could not help thinking

00:07:10.470 --> 00:07:12.450
that he was a local xxx staff

00:07:12.450 --> 00:07:15.510
working for the lawyers office.

00:07:15.510 --> 00:07:18.060
Only after our conversation
a few minutes later,

00:07:18.060 --> 00:07:19.293
I realized I was wrong.

00:07:20.400 --> 00:07:22.550
Junior is a refugees
registered with UNHCR,

00:07:23.640 --> 00:07:26.010
who has come to live in
Thailand since he was a boy

00:07:26.010 --> 00:07:28.320
together with his family of six.

00:07:28.320 --> 00:07:30.823
He was the second child
of the Hmong pastor.

00:07:30.823 --> 00:07:32.190
He fled to Thailand

00:07:32.190 --> 00:07:35.370
due to religious persecution
in northern Vietnam.

00:07:35.370 --> 00:07:36.510
His Thai was flawless

00:07:36.510 --> 00:07:39.390
because he did go to
the Thai primary school

00:07:39.390 --> 00:07:42.363
before quitting to help
earn income for his family.

00:07:44.280 --> 00:07:46.470
Junior also speaks Thai with his siblings

00:07:46.470 --> 00:07:49.380
and friends in the same
community and at home.

00:07:49.380 --> 00:07:50.610
However, Junior has continued

00:07:50.610 --> 00:07:53.550
to be able to speak his
native tongue, Hmong,

00:07:53.550 --> 00:07:55.800
he also learned English at a church

00:07:55.800 --> 00:07:58.353
and his father's encouraging
him to study Chinese.

00:07:59.220 --> 00:08:02.580
His language skills landed
him a job as a translator

00:08:02.580 --> 00:08:04.800
for various refugee organizations

00:08:04.800 --> 00:08:07.353
where he continued to work all the days.

00:08:08.520 --> 00:08:10.800
Since our first encounter,

00:08:10.800 --> 00:08:13.380
I have become a frequent
visitors to Junior's family

00:08:13.380 --> 00:08:14.970
and his community.

00:08:14.970 --> 00:08:17.610
I participated in some
of their activities.

00:08:17.610 --> 00:08:21.420
I learned several remarkable
insights from their experiences

00:08:21.420 --> 00:08:23.340
and livelihoods in Bangkok.

00:08:23.340 --> 00:08:27.150
And let me mention to you,
Junior's father, the pastor,

00:08:27.150 --> 00:08:29.790
is at the core of the public community.

00:08:29.790 --> 00:08:32.040
He's not only a spiritual guide,

00:08:32.040 --> 00:08:34.680
but also a community
leader who plays a role

00:08:34.680 --> 00:08:37.290
in structuring the xxx community.

00:08:37.290 --> 00:08:38.820
At the end of Sunday mass,

00:08:38.820 --> 00:08:40.140
he often took the opportunity

00:08:40.140 --> 00:08:42.393
to foster community spirit and unity.

00:08:43.768 --> 00:08:45.570
In the absence of justice in Thailand

00:08:45.570 --> 00:08:47.733
due to their lack of legal status.

00:08:49.018 --> 00:08:51.750
In the absence of access to
justice xxx, my apologies,

00:08:51.750 --> 00:08:53.790
the pastor and some key community members

00:08:53.790 --> 00:08:57.480
serve as the elders who
adjudicate community conflicts

00:08:57.480 --> 00:08:59.400
and familiar disputes.

00:08:59.400 --> 00:09:01.320
The elders also play an essential role

00:09:01.320 --> 00:09:04.950
in bringing employment
opportunities to community members.

00:09:04.950 --> 00:09:07.140
They leverage their social network

00:09:07.140 --> 00:09:09.030
primarily to the Hmong people

00:09:09.030 --> 00:09:11.760
who have already been living
in Thailand for decades,

00:09:11.760 --> 00:09:15.300
and from the previous employers
to get people to work.

00:09:15.300 --> 00:09:16.713
Not everyone is employed.

00:09:18.360 --> 00:09:21.570
Although they can get
jobs, I must note that

00:09:21.570 --> 00:09:24.210
these jobs are available
in the informal sectors

00:09:24.210 --> 00:09:26.250
with no legal contracts.

00:09:26.250 --> 00:09:29.883
These includes a translation
job that Junior has as well.

00:09:30.990 --> 00:09:32.970
The home of the pastors and the church

00:09:32.970 --> 00:09:34.950
serve as a community centers

00:09:34.950 --> 00:09:37.440
where community activities occur.

00:09:37.440 --> 00:09:39.990
Before the COVID-19 pandemic,

00:09:39.990 --> 00:09:42.810
while adults and teens
would go to mass on Sunday,

00:09:42.810 --> 00:09:44.520
the second floor of the home of the pastor

00:09:44.520 --> 00:09:46.170
would turn into nurseries

00:09:46.170 --> 00:09:47.940
where volunteers would
come and teach English

00:09:47.940 --> 00:09:48.933
to young kids.

00:09:49.770 --> 00:09:51.600
The first floor of the house

00:09:51.600 --> 00:09:54.030
also serve as a community workshop

00:09:54.030 --> 00:09:57.570
where ladies would go and
engage in making handcrafts

00:09:57.570 --> 00:10:00.150
and products like this.

00:10:00.150 --> 00:10:03.630
Some of these are sold through
the CHAMALiiN projects,

00:10:03.630 --> 00:10:06.030
established by local refugee organizations

00:10:06.030 --> 00:10:08.313
to empower female refugees.

00:10:10.860 --> 00:10:12.720
Despite opportunities,

00:10:12.720 --> 00:10:14.640
the Hmong communities also face challenges

00:10:14.640 --> 00:10:17.070
because of they say illegally in Thailand.

00:10:17.070 --> 00:10:20.610
Rates and arrests still
affect community members.

00:10:20.610 --> 00:10:22.170
However, in many instance,

00:10:22.170 --> 00:10:24.330
Thai neighbors help shield them

00:10:24.330 --> 00:10:26.730
by providing informations
about police monitoring

00:10:26.730 --> 00:10:27.563
in the area,

00:10:29.484 --> 00:10:33.330
so they will not be subjected
to arrest and detentions.

00:10:33.330 --> 00:10:35.550
During the COVID-19 pandemic,

00:10:35.550 --> 00:10:37.980
the informal structures of the community

00:10:37.980 --> 00:10:39.600
also play a vital role

00:10:39.600 --> 00:10:42.310
in spreading informations
about COVID-19 interventions

00:10:43.260 --> 00:10:45.753
and helping manage those getting infected.

00:10:47.190 --> 00:10:49.470
And this is the pictures
from the mass at the church

00:10:49.470 --> 00:10:51.303
that they gathered every Sunday.

00:10:55.380 --> 00:10:57.000
Some part of the stories,

00:10:57.000 --> 00:10:59.250
such as the existence
of the community elders

00:10:59.250 --> 00:11:01.350
may be unique to the Hmong community.

00:11:01.350 --> 00:11:04.740
However, based on interviews
that my team and I conducted,

00:11:04.740 --> 00:11:08.850
the refugees from 10
nationalities in 10 communities,

00:11:08.850 --> 00:11:12.360
the overall experiences of
refugees are strikingly similar,

00:11:12.360 --> 00:11:15.390
ranging from the stories of
their journeys into Thailand,

00:11:15.390 --> 00:11:17.253
people overstaying their visa,

00:11:18.150 --> 00:11:20.760
and get to stay in Thailand more longer

00:11:20.760 --> 00:11:23.100
to the stories of
pathway to get employment

00:11:23.100 --> 00:11:25.890
and their experience of urban institution.

00:11:25.890 --> 00:11:26.970
The story I just told you

00:11:26.970 --> 00:11:30.570
may also resonate with
urban refugee experiences

00:11:30.570 --> 00:11:32.640
in other countries as well.

00:11:32.640 --> 00:11:36.843
If these stories are so common,
why do I still mention them?

00:11:38.190 --> 00:11:40.080
I mention them because these stories

00:11:40.080 --> 00:11:42.510
bring me to the key
puzzles for my talk today.

00:11:42.510 --> 00:11:45.060
How is refugee asylums
in Thailand possible

00:11:45.060 --> 00:11:48.210
despite the hostile legal environments?

00:11:48.210 --> 00:11:52.650
How could we explain these phenomenon?

00:11:52.650 --> 00:11:55.080
There are various factors of course.

00:11:55.080 --> 00:11:57.690
Elsewhere, I did explain the
role of the social network,

00:11:57.690 --> 00:12:00.060
the importance of the
Thai government policies

00:12:00.060 --> 00:12:03.810
and the existence of refugee
organizations and UNHCR office.

00:12:03.810 --> 00:12:08.190
But what about the space where
they live, like the city?

00:12:08.190 --> 00:12:12.120
How does the city shape and
make refugee asylums possible?

00:12:12.120 --> 00:12:13.980
How should we understand the connection

00:12:13.980 --> 00:12:16.920
between the cities and refugees,

00:12:16.920 --> 00:12:19.350
especially their abilities
to navigate livelihoods

00:12:19.350 --> 00:12:23.583
in the cities without
legal status and rights?

00:12:24.420 --> 00:12:26.460
Personally, I think refugee scholars

00:12:26.460 --> 00:12:27.690
have taken the connections

00:12:27.690 --> 00:12:31.170
between the cities and refugees
for granted and too likely.

00:12:31.170 --> 00:12:33.810
They focus excessively on
the relative advantages

00:12:33.810 --> 00:12:37.290
that the cities bring to refugees
compared to those in camp,

00:12:37.290 --> 00:12:41.370
such as free mobilities,
employment opportunities.

00:12:41.370 --> 00:12:43.590
I do not deny these explanations,

00:12:43.590 --> 00:12:45.630
but I believe that we can better theorize

00:12:45.630 --> 00:12:47.520
the refugees cities nexus

00:12:47.520 --> 00:12:49.860
and this is what exactly
I'm trying to accomplish

00:12:49.860 --> 00:12:52.560
by the end of of this talk today.

00:12:52.560 --> 00:12:55.320
Therefore, I am trying
to bring urban studies

00:12:55.320 --> 00:12:57.903
to contribute to refugee
studies in my talk,

00:12:59.190 --> 00:13:02.190
to explain how refugees
constructed asylums

00:13:02.190 --> 00:13:03.723
in non-signatory states.

00:13:04.590 --> 00:13:09.277
My argument is as follow, "The
city's three forms of powers,

00:13:09.277 --> 00:13:12.727
"including environmental,
discursive and material powers,

00:13:12.727 --> 00:13:15.067
"have bolstered the resilience of refugees

00:13:15.067 --> 00:13:17.827
"by providing them with
three essential capacities

00:13:17.827 --> 00:13:19.537
"for city navigations.

00:13:19.537 --> 00:13:21.487
"And through which it's allowed

00:13:21.487 --> 00:13:24.937
"the refugees to be able
to construct asylums

00:13:24.937 --> 00:13:28.377
"even in the context of a
non-signatory states by Thailand."

00:13:29.760 --> 00:13:32.310
I do now introduce two
interrelated concept,

00:13:32.310 --> 00:13:36.150
which is central to my
arguments and my explanations,

00:13:36.150 --> 00:13:38.463
urban power and refugee resilience.

00:13:40.290 --> 00:13:41.700
Urban power.

00:13:41.700 --> 00:13:45.000
For those of you who study urban
politics and urban studies,

00:13:45.000 --> 00:13:46.233
the xxx.

00:13:47.070 --> 00:13:50.730
For decades, we have seen the
emergence of other discussions

00:13:50.730 --> 00:13:52.200
about urban power.

00:13:52.200 --> 00:13:54.540
For example, in the
famous book "Who Governs,"

00:13:54.540 --> 00:13:57.030
by Robert Dahl who taught here at Yale,

00:13:57.030 --> 00:13:59.430
and brought about the cities of New Haven,

00:13:59.430 --> 00:14:02.640
also led us to understand
urban powered structure.

00:14:02.640 --> 00:14:04.800
From there, we also seen
the growing literatures

00:14:04.800 --> 00:14:07.890
on urban power relations,
power in the cities,

00:14:07.890 --> 00:14:10.640
or even "Cities of Power,"
which is the title of a book

00:14:11.856 --> 00:14:15.300
by Therborne, that just
came out couple years ago.

00:14:15.300 --> 00:14:17.670
Power in the Cities continue
to be a recurring theme

00:14:17.670 --> 00:14:19.410
in urban affairs nowadays,

00:14:19.410 --> 00:14:20.790
although some have moved on

00:14:20.790 --> 00:14:22.990
to talk about urban
governance, for example.

00:14:23.880 --> 00:14:25.320
On the other hand,

00:14:25.320 --> 00:14:28.920
urban power is also
discussed among sociologists

00:14:28.920 --> 00:14:31.740
and geographer who have
looked into urban powers

00:14:31.740 --> 00:14:34.230
from a slightly different perspective.

00:14:34.230 --> 00:14:37.410
This seem to focus more on
the power of place and space,

00:14:37.410 --> 00:14:39.240
and the power of the cities.

00:14:39.240 --> 00:14:43.110
And how these shape social
relations and interactions.

00:14:43.110 --> 00:14:46.020
We often hear them proclaim xxx matters.

00:14:46.020 --> 00:14:49.440
We have seen the classic
sociological work of Sampson,

00:14:49.440 --> 00:14:52.320
on "Neighborhood effects,"
which attempts to explain

00:14:52.320 --> 00:14:55.350
how the neighborhoods can
be an essential determinant

00:14:55.350 --> 00:14:57.243
of socioeconomic stratifications.

00:14:58.260 --> 00:15:02.670
For me, urban power relations
as well as social interactions

00:15:02.670 --> 00:15:05.514
are quite crucial for
the construction of life

00:15:05.514 --> 00:15:07.380
and of the cities.

00:15:07.380 --> 00:15:10.740
I look into the world of a
geographer like Robert Sack,

00:15:10.740 --> 00:15:13.620
who argue that the
power of place and space

00:15:13.620 --> 00:15:17.250
comes only from the xxx rules

00:15:17.250 --> 00:15:20.370
about what is in and our place.

00:15:20.370 --> 00:15:23.250
But again, who make those rules.

00:15:23.250 --> 00:15:24.960
It is those who govern that place

00:15:24.960 --> 00:15:28.410
and perhaps those who comes
from social interactions

00:15:28.410 --> 00:15:30.917
that give meaning to such a place.

00:15:30.917 --> 00:15:31.750
For this reason,

00:15:31.750 --> 00:15:35.880
these multiple social,
political, and economic relations

00:15:35.880 --> 00:15:38.610
eventually lend power to the city.

00:15:38.610 --> 00:15:42.300
It was from this urban power
that new arrivals, for example,

00:15:42.300 --> 00:15:45.660
migrants or refugees could
derive their resilience

00:15:45.660 --> 00:15:48.024
and integrate into the cities.

00:15:48.024 --> 00:15:51.570
Because there are multiple
forms of relations

00:15:51.570 --> 00:15:53.490
and governance in the cities,

00:15:53.490 --> 00:15:55.863
there can be various form of urban power.

00:15:56.730 --> 00:15:59.190
Today my talks will focus
on only three forms,

00:15:59.190 --> 00:16:02.493
environmental, discursive
and material powers.

00:16:03.690 --> 00:16:07.140
Other urban powers can be
elusive and so abstracts.

00:16:07.140 --> 00:16:09.150
I believe it would be
worthwhile to defy it

00:16:09.150 --> 00:16:13.500
as a form of power embedded in the cities

00:16:13.500 --> 00:16:15.570
emerging from complex relations

00:16:15.570 --> 00:16:18.120
that shape the dynamics of the cities

00:16:18.120 --> 00:16:19.623
and the city constructions.

00:16:20.940 --> 00:16:24.150
Second, the term refugee resilience.

00:16:24.150 --> 00:16:26.160
From a rather traditional concepts,

00:16:26.160 --> 00:16:28.560
that I introduced just now,
I would like to move on

00:16:28.560 --> 00:16:31.410
to discuss a more
fashionable term resilience,

00:16:31.410 --> 00:16:34.890
which has been used
widely in various fields.

00:16:34.890 --> 00:16:38.190
Generally the terms refer
to positive adaptations

00:16:38.190 --> 00:16:40.830
and capabilities to
recover from challenges

00:16:40.830 --> 00:16:42.750
and setbacks quickly.

00:16:42.750 --> 00:16:46.530
Some scholars even treat
resilience as a paradigm.

00:16:46.530 --> 00:16:49.950
In refugee studies, resilience
is also very popular,

00:16:49.950 --> 00:16:52.500
especially among those
studying refugee wellbeing

00:16:52.500 --> 00:16:56.250
and those focusing on livelihood
adjustment and strategies.

00:16:56.250 --> 00:16:59.520
My usage of the term is
also pretty straightforward.

00:16:59.520 --> 00:17:03.270
I adopt these terms because
I see it as more encompassing.

00:17:03.270 --> 00:17:04.890
For me, it is suitable,

00:17:04.890 --> 00:17:06.480
because when we talk about resilience

00:17:06.480 --> 00:17:09.150
we can look into two
interconnected components

00:17:09.150 --> 00:17:10.530
of capacities.

00:17:10.530 --> 00:17:12.870
The first is to have capacities

00:17:12.870 --> 00:17:14.340
and the second is connected

00:17:14.340 --> 00:17:16.680
to the abilities to use such capacities

00:17:16.680 --> 00:17:18.840
to make difference in the situations,

00:17:18.840 --> 00:17:21.180
and life situations in particular.

00:17:21.180 --> 00:17:22.590
With the concept of resilience,

00:17:22.590 --> 00:17:24.180
it is also important to note,

00:17:24.180 --> 00:17:28.500
that we can give agency
back to refugee themselves,

00:17:28.500 --> 00:17:32.400
as many states often
take that away from them

00:17:32.400 --> 00:17:35.940
and put them into a positions
a burden or dependence.

00:17:35.940 --> 00:17:38.430
In conceptualizing resilience,

00:17:38.430 --> 00:17:40.080
most literatures would focus more

00:17:40.080 --> 00:17:41.883
on resilience that come from within,

00:17:43.542 --> 00:17:46.650
meaning to look into their
mental adjustment in itself

00:17:46.650 --> 00:17:49.050
and available resources
and social network.

00:17:49.050 --> 00:17:51.300
But for me, when we
think about resilience,

00:17:51.300 --> 00:17:53.370
or refugees resilience in particular,

00:17:53.370 --> 00:17:56.100
especially in the context
of urban refugees,

00:17:56.100 --> 00:17:59.430
resilience also come from the city itself.

00:17:59.430 --> 00:18:03.090
Different forms of urban power
provide various capacities

00:18:03.090 --> 00:18:05.040
and in my talk you will see,

00:18:05.040 --> 00:18:07.773
which I'm gonna show you
later on in the table.

00:18:10.110 --> 00:18:13.320
From which thoughts these
resilience are coming from.

00:18:13.320 --> 00:18:16.653
Different form of urban power
provide various capacities.

00:18:17.490 --> 00:18:20.190
And to repeat my arguments
again, the CD3 forms,

00:18:20.190 --> 00:18:21.870
which including material, discursive,

00:18:21.870 --> 00:18:24.573
and environmental powers,
they have bolsters,

00:18:25.650 --> 00:18:28.923
materials, discursive, and
environmental capacities.

00:18:30.060 --> 00:18:34.290
Now that I have laid foundations
for my conceptual framework

00:18:34.290 --> 00:18:35.340
for the subject matters,

00:18:35.340 --> 00:18:38.310
please allow me to delve into
details for each one of them

00:18:38.310 --> 00:18:41.370
and then also to provide you
with some empirical evidence.

00:18:41.370 --> 00:18:43.470
The empirical evidence
which provide insights

00:18:43.470 --> 00:18:44.550
into what I'm presenting today

00:18:44.550 --> 00:18:46.380
is come from ethnographic field work

00:18:46.380 --> 00:18:48.030
that have been conducted in Thailand

00:18:48.030 --> 00:18:49.320
for the past three years,

00:18:49.320 --> 00:18:53.310
observing communities
activities and engage with them,

00:18:53.310 --> 00:18:57.300
having conversations with
those ends of local CSOs

00:18:57.300 --> 00:19:00.180
and also more than 140 interviews

00:19:00.180 --> 00:19:04.482
that my team and I conducted
with refugees back in 2020s.

00:19:04.482 --> 00:19:06.960
These interviews were
conducted as part of attempts

00:19:06.960 --> 00:19:09.900
to understand refugee
integration in Thailand,

00:19:09.900 --> 00:19:12.090
and it was conducted via telephone.

00:19:12.090 --> 00:19:14.130
We adopt this method since at that time

00:19:14.130 --> 00:19:15.840
it was the beginning of the pandemic,

00:19:15.840 --> 00:19:19.260
and we were not able to
get into the community.

00:19:19.260 --> 00:19:21.300
They were conducted over
the period of four months

00:19:21.300 --> 00:19:24.240
between Septembers and December 2020s,

00:19:24.240 --> 00:19:26.550
and a team of five
people, including myself,

00:19:26.550 --> 00:19:28.863
we engaged in this interview all together.

00:19:30.720 --> 00:19:33.660
Now going into detail
for each one of them,

00:19:33.660 --> 00:19:36.990
these are the form of urban
powers I'm talking about

00:19:36.990 --> 00:19:38.790
and the form of resilience

00:19:38.790 --> 00:19:41.613
that correspond with
different urban powers.

00:19:44.041 --> 00:19:46.440
I will start first with the
first form of urban power,

00:19:46.440 --> 00:19:48.450
which is the urban environmental powers,

00:19:48.450 --> 00:19:52.050
which is corresponding with
the environmental capacities.

00:19:52.050 --> 00:19:54.930
When hearing the term
environmental powers,

00:19:54.930 --> 00:19:57.240
people often go back to the the work

00:19:57.240 --> 00:20:00.300
and the thinking of Thorstein Veblen,

00:20:00.300 --> 00:20:02.190
who argue in the Theory
of the Leisure Class

00:20:02.190 --> 00:20:04.440
that environmental power emerge

00:20:04.440 --> 00:20:07.740
from how people demonstrate
their command of the environment

00:20:07.740 --> 00:20:09.960
to their wealth and social positions.

00:20:09.960 --> 00:20:10.860
Environmental power

00:20:10.860 --> 00:20:13.473
therefore would become an
indicator of social power.

00:20:14.730 --> 00:20:18.750
Based on Veblen's insights,
these advantages, people,

00:20:18.750 --> 00:20:20.730
may also have environmental power

00:20:20.730 --> 00:20:23.670
if we think of this in the relative terms,

00:20:23.670 --> 00:20:26.430
but that power would be
lesses to the wealthier

00:20:26.430 --> 00:20:28.860
and those with higher social positions.

00:20:28.860 --> 00:20:31.980
However, the critical
questions we also have to ask,

00:20:31.980 --> 00:20:35.070
it's where is environmental
powers comes from?

00:20:35.070 --> 00:20:39.390
Well, you need access
to the environment first

00:20:39.390 --> 00:20:41.310
to be able to command it.

00:20:41.310 --> 00:20:42.540
If that's the case,

00:20:42.540 --> 00:20:45.330
then this environmental
power is not simply related,

00:20:45.330 --> 00:20:49.050
but come from human interactions
with the environment

00:20:49.050 --> 00:20:51.840
to amend or chain the landscapes,

00:20:51.840 --> 00:20:55.560
build new structures through
the social interactions

00:20:55.560 --> 00:20:57.720
that human has with the environment.

00:20:57.720 --> 00:20:58.650
It is unsurprising

00:20:58.650 --> 00:21:03.360
that powers has also led to
the restructures environment.

00:21:03.360 --> 00:21:05.303
Also giving the environment
the so-called power

00:21:05.303 --> 00:21:10.303
as well in turn, which can
then be learned to anyone,

00:21:10.350 --> 00:21:13.530
including those with
less power than refugees.

00:21:13.530 --> 00:21:17.280
Let me put this in more concrete
terms of urban refugees.

00:21:17.280 --> 00:21:20.070
When I'm talking about the
environment in this context,

00:21:20.070 --> 00:21:22.890
I'm talking mainly about
the built environment.

00:21:22.890 --> 00:21:26.730
This is all the physical
parts of where we live, rest,

00:21:26.730 --> 00:21:30.120
work and perform or engage
in various activities

00:21:30.120 --> 00:21:32.460
in a city such as Bangkok.

00:21:32.460 --> 00:21:34.800
The built environment is complex.

00:21:34.800 --> 00:21:36.270
Buildings become a fortress

00:21:36.270 --> 00:21:39.120
where people can shoot
themselves from one another.

00:21:39.120 --> 00:21:41.220
These form a social
interactions between humans

00:21:41.220 --> 00:21:42.510
and the built environment,

00:21:42.510 --> 00:21:46.050
made the environmental power
of the city very powerful.

00:21:46.050 --> 00:21:48.360
Authority cannot penetrate all areas

00:21:48.360 --> 00:21:49.620
unless there are reports

00:21:49.620 --> 00:21:52.080
from those living in
those built environment.

00:21:52.080 --> 00:21:53.550
People can become invisible

00:21:53.550 --> 00:21:56.283
behind the concrete balls
in their private apartment.

00:21:57.810 --> 00:22:01.110
These former urban powers
therefore left refugees

00:22:01.110 --> 00:22:04.380
the environmental capacities
to make a decision

00:22:04.380 --> 00:22:07.170
to command how they will
use the built environment

00:22:07.170 --> 00:22:12.170
to xxx, or enhance other
social and economic benefits.

00:22:12.660 --> 00:22:16.260
Usually they will prioritize safety, why?

00:22:16.260 --> 00:22:18.270
As I mentioned before,

00:22:18.270 --> 00:22:21.750
refugees are considered
illegal migrants in Thailand.

00:22:21.750 --> 00:22:24.450
They can be arrested and detained.

00:22:24.450 --> 00:22:27.720
For example, one Palestinian family I visited,

00:22:27.720 --> 00:22:31.350
will close their house
door most of the time,

00:22:31.350 --> 00:22:33.420
whether they're inside or not.

00:22:33.420 --> 00:22:35.737
Sabiha always said to me,

00:22:35.737 --> 00:22:38.137
"We always feel safer here in this house

00:22:38.137 --> 00:22:39.660
"when we close the door."

00:22:39.660 --> 00:22:41.490
But actually the side of the house itself

00:22:41.490 --> 00:22:44.100
is also a very safe place

00:22:44.100 --> 00:22:46.620
because it's located in
the gated communities

00:22:46.620 --> 00:22:48.210
of most Thai people.

00:22:48.210 --> 00:22:50.010
But being in a commodity environment

00:22:50.010 --> 00:22:53.670
made refugees feel a double
layers of cell protections.

00:22:53.670 --> 00:22:54.503
The housing choice

00:22:54.503 --> 00:22:57.570
is also related to their access to a xxx

00:22:57.570 --> 00:22:58.893
and also job sites.

00:22:59.730 --> 00:23:01.440
However, is essential to note that

00:23:01.440 --> 00:23:04.740
when a number of refugees
moving into community

00:23:04.740 --> 00:23:09.090
and other areas together,
the city environmental powers

00:23:09.090 --> 00:23:13.020
may hinder the refugees
environmental capacities as well.

00:23:13.020 --> 00:23:16.080
There were several incidents
of refugees getting arrested

00:23:16.080 --> 00:23:19.080
because of the neighborhood
reported a high concentrations

00:23:19.080 --> 00:23:21.810
of foreigners or refugees
living in the areas

00:23:21.810 --> 00:23:22.983
to local police.

00:23:25.650 --> 00:23:27.660
The second form of urban power

00:23:27.660 --> 00:23:30.123
that I will be discussing
is discursive power.

00:23:33.188 --> 00:23:36.810
This form of urban powers emerged
from the city's densities,

00:23:36.810 --> 00:23:40.710
varieties, and diversities
of people, places and space.

00:23:40.710 --> 00:23:42.900
The city has posted this power

00:23:42.900 --> 00:23:45.540
perhaps since the city start to expand.

00:23:45.540 --> 00:23:50.340
We can trace back to the work
of Wirth on Urbanisms in 1938,

00:23:50.340 --> 00:23:51.780
where he talks about

00:23:51.780 --> 00:23:54.570
the heterogeneity of people in the cities

00:23:54.570 --> 00:23:56.790
and the later works of
other urban sociologists.

00:23:56.790 --> 00:23:59.910
We have discussed
anonymities in the cities.

00:23:59.910 --> 00:24:03.120
What does it mean to the
capacity of the people?

00:24:03.120 --> 00:24:05.850
The city provide capacities for refugees

00:24:05.850 --> 00:24:10.320
to move in and out xxx
depending on place and space.

00:24:10.320 --> 00:24:12.210
In the context of this discursive powers,

00:24:12.210 --> 00:24:14.520
particularly in the context of Thailand,

00:24:14.520 --> 00:24:16.740
I'm talking about the
new categories of people

00:24:16.740 --> 00:24:20.160
label as refugee migrants.

00:24:20.160 --> 00:24:22.020
Because of Thailand is a non-xxx state,

00:24:22.020 --> 00:24:23.880
as I mentioned before, and always,

00:24:23.880 --> 00:24:26.640
refugees are not recognized as refugees.

00:24:26.640 --> 00:24:28.980
Even those with UNHCR cards,

00:24:28.980 --> 00:24:31.660
they are known as a person
of concerns to UNHCR

00:24:32.610 --> 00:24:35.310
and they prefer to identify
themselves as migrants,

00:24:35.310 --> 00:24:38.550
especially to the locals
who are potential employers.

00:24:38.550 --> 00:24:40.050
The urban discursive powers

00:24:40.050 --> 00:24:42.380
therefore allow refugees to xxx and xxx

00:24:43.230 --> 00:24:44.700
their own stories,

00:24:44.700 --> 00:24:47.340
making them in command of their labels.

00:24:47.340 --> 00:24:50.913
This is precisely what
xxx say that refugees,

00:24:51.817 --> 00:24:54.157
"Have a very context dependence

00:24:54.157 --> 00:24:56.517
"and pragmatic relationship
to their labels."

00:24:58.170 --> 00:25:02.550
In extreme cases, that's
what I mentioned already.

00:25:02.550 --> 00:25:05.520
In extreme cases, urban discursive powers

00:25:05.520 --> 00:25:08.910
also allows some refugees to pass as Thai.

00:25:08.910 --> 00:25:10.560
Passing refers to the process

00:25:10.560 --> 00:25:13.800
by which an individual can cross over

00:25:13.800 --> 00:25:18.210
from one identity to another
based on specific attributes.

00:25:18.210 --> 00:25:20.580
In the case of urban refugees in Thailand,

00:25:20.580 --> 00:25:22.500
that is the Thai language, xxx,

00:25:22.500 --> 00:25:25.140
combined with a similar
physical appearance.

00:25:25.140 --> 00:25:27.613
Passing is a forceful form
of discursive capacities,

00:25:27.613 --> 00:25:31.320
that urban refugee used
to avoid getting arrests

00:25:31.320 --> 00:25:33.360
if police encountered them on the street

00:25:33.360 --> 00:25:34.760
or at the construction site.

00:25:36.390 --> 00:25:39.007
The Hmong refugees for example,
mentioned to be in quote,

00:25:39.007 --> 00:25:41.973
"We look like Thai people
and speak the language well,

00:25:43.887 --> 00:25:46.557
"so we can get away with
many things in Thailand."

00:25:49.080 --> 00:25:52.590
Last but not least, material powers,

00:25:52.590 --> 00:25:55.080
which does not need much explanations.

00:25:55.080 --> 00:25:57.870
This is perhaps the most
widely discussed connections

00:25:57.870 --> 00:25:59.970
between refugees and the cities.

00:25:59.970 --> 00:26:01.427
The urban material powers

00:26:01.427 --> 00:26:04.380
emerge from the economic
relations in the cities.

00:26:04.380 --> 00:26:08.040
We have seen multiple
economics areas of the cities,

00:26:08.040 --> 00:26:09.900
either it being the site productions

00:26:09.900 --> 00:26:11.047
or the locus xxx.

00:26:12.780 --> 00:26:16.020
Economic relation helps
structures urban material powers.

00:26:16.020 --> 00:26:18.870
This power in turn
structures social relations

00:26:18.870 --> 00:26:21.750
between economic agents, namely employers,

00:26:21.750 --> 00:26:22.983
and also employees.

00:26:24.150 --> 00:26:26.090
What the city use to urban refugees

00:26:26.090 --> 00:26:30.390
in the case of Thailand is
capacities in the economic realm

00:26:30.390 --> 00:26:32.040
or material capacities,

00:26:32.040 --> 00:26:34.170
which allows them to navigate employment

00:26:34.170 --> 00:26:37.650
and jobs opportunities and
acquire material needs.

00:26:37.650 --> 00:26:39.120
Bank of Thailand approximates

00:26:39.120 --> 00:26:43.470
that size of the high informal
economies between 45 to 50%,

00:26:43.470 --> 00:26:45.870
and has xxx more than 60%

00:26:45.870 --> 00:26:48.390
of the workforce in the countries.

00:26:48.390 --> 00:26:50.880
Bangkok as the country capitals,

00:26:50.880 --> 00:26:53.730
and economic and financial
center of the countries,

00:26:53.730 --> 00:26:57.240
also serve as a major hub
for the informal sectors.

00:26:57.240 --> 00:26:59.850
Our interviews review
that Bangkok has provided

00:26:59.850 --> 00:27:02.940
various economic
opportunities for refugees.

00:27:02.940 --> 00:27:06.030
The extent to which they
could acquire those chances

00:27:06.030 --> 00:27:09.660
also varies depending on
their skill, experiences,

00:27:09.660 --> 00:27:11.520
network and connections.

00:27:11.520 --> 00:27:13.140
But let me show you
some datas that we filed

00:27:13.140 --> 00:27:14.973
from 140 interview that we did.

00:27:15.990 --> 00:27:19.080
74% of the 140 respondents

00:27:19.080 --> 00:27:21.180
were engaged in income
gathering activities

00:27:21.180 --> 00:27:24.150
in the informal sectors
before the pandemic.

00:27:24.150 --> 00:27:27.660
20% of all the respondents who
reported working in Thailand

00:27:27.660 --> 00:27:29.760
had worked in at least two jobs.

00:27:29.760 --> 00:27:31.470
And respondent wages varies.

00:27:31.470 --> 00:27:33.960
Some wages could be as low as $1 per day

00:27:33.960 --> 00:27:35.820
for picking dry chili tips

00:27:35.820 --> 00:27:38.250
to others receiving up to 800

00:27:38.250 --> 00:27:42.210
or around 20 US dollars per
day working in organizations,

00:27:42.210 --> 00:27:44.700
depending on their skills required.

00:27:44.700 --> 00:27:46.770
Those low wages perhaps explain

00:27:46.770 --> 00:27:48.670
why they are working in multiple jobs.

00:27:50.040 --> 00:27:52.560
These datas that are
presented to you here,

00:27:52.560 --> 00:27:54.270
show not only what a city

00:27:54.270 --> 00:27:57.240
like Bangkok can provide
for urban refugees,

00:27:57.240 --> 00:28:01.530
but also the extent urban
refugees have already contributed

00:28:01.530 --> 00:28:05.040
with their material
capacities to the Thai economy

00:28:05.040 --> 00:28:09.660
and the cities, how the city
urban power enabled them

00:28:09.660 --> 00:28:12.510
to have these particular capacities.

00:28:12.510 --> 00:28:14.970
However, the contributions
of urban refugees in Bangkok

00:28:14.970 --> 00:28:15.803
xxx.

00:28:17.370 --> 00:28:18.900
Up to this point,

00:28:18.900 --> 00:28:20.910
I have already provided
a detailed explanations

00:28:20.910 --> 00:28:21.743
of the interactions

00:28:21.743 --> 00:28:24.300
between urban powers
and refugee resilience,

00:28:24.300 --> 00:28:28.170
focusing on three forms
of capacities as xxx.

00:28:28.170 --> 00:28:29.640
And these urban powers

00:28:29.640 --> 00:28:32.160
and the corresponding forms
of refugee capacities,

00:28:32.160 --> 00:28:35.070
which I frame them as
a refugees resilience

00:28:35.070 --> 00:28:37.500
help make asylum possible
for urban refugees,

00:28:37.500 --> 00:28:40.593
even in the context of a
non-signatory states like Thailand.

00:28:41.760 --> 00:28:43.710
Auto-focusing on both factors,

00:28:43.710 --> 00:28:45.510
I just want to repeat it again

00:28:45.510 --> 00:28:48.630
that I recognize the significance
of some other factors

00:28:48.630 --> 00:28:51.450
that I mentioned at the
beginning of my talk.

00:28:51.450 --> 00:28:53.043
Before I end this talk,

00:28:55.710 --> 00:28:57.660
I would like to address one final issues

00:28:57.660 --> 00:29:01.230
regarding the sustainabilities of asylums.

00:29:01.230 --> 00:29:05.100
Auto city like Bangkok can
provide xxx for refugees.

00:29:05.100 --> 00:29:07.890
The fact that refugees
can settle their asylums

00:29:07.890 --> 00:29:11.680
with urban powers and resilience
has also become a barriers

00:29:13.230 --> 00:29:16.860
for their onward movement
and limbo as a refugees.

00:29:16.860 --> 00:29:19.650
Let me now revisit the stories
of the Hmong communities

00:29:19.650 --> 00:29:20.493
that I started.

00:29:21.330 --> 00:29:25.920
In June 2019, I visited
Junior's family as usual,

00:29:25.920 --> 00:29:27.240
and this time I had chance

00:29:27.240 --> 00:29:31.980
to discuss with his father,
pastor, for almost two hours.

00:29:31.980 --> 00:29:35.700
He told me that his families
face resettlement challenges.

00:29:35.700 --> 00:29:37.800
A sponsor in Canada told the pastor

00:29:37.800 --> 00:29:42.800
that he wants to sponsor someone
else who is really in need

00:29:42.960 --> 00:29:46.443
since the pastor's family seems
to be better off in Bangkok.

00:29:47.490 --> 00:29:49.653
The pastor told me with low voice,

00:29:51.157 --> 00:29:54.307
"I was quite surprised
that he said that to me.

00:29:54.307 --> 00:29:57.487
"We are indeed better off
economically in Bangkok,

00:29:57.487 --> 00:29:59.673
"but that is what we must do to survive.

00:30:01.747 --> 00:30:05.437
"It also doesn't mean we
won't need protections.

00:30:05.437 --> 00:30:08.007
"The Thai government didn't
recognize our status."

00:30:10.081 --> 00:30:13.440
Because of the resettlement uncertainties,

00:30:13.440 --> 00:30:16.410
the pastor's currently
exploring other options

00:30:16.410 --> 00:30:17.940
for his families.

00:30:17.940 --> 00:30:22.590
One of them was to have
Junior or another of his sons

00:30:22.590 --> 00:30:26.220
get resettled first either
in Canada or somewhere else.

00:30:26.220 --> 00:30:28.050
He's also preparing for the worse

00:30:28.050 --> 00:30:30.090
if the families and the communities

00:30:30.090 --> 00:30:33.120
must continue living in
Thailand for years to come.

00:30:33.120 --> 00:30:34.870
And he saying, "God, (indistinct)."

00:30:38.640 --> 00:30:41.040
Being able to seek asylum is one thing,

00:30:41.040 --> 00:30:43.140
but moving to a third country is another,

00:30:43.140 --> 00:30:44.970
and the latters continue to be the hope

00:30:44.970 --> 00:30:47.460
of nearly all urban refugees in Thailand,

00:30:47.460 --> 00:30:48.930
despite knowing that the chance

00:30:48.930 --> 00:30:51.210
has become increasingly limited.

00:30:51.210 --> 00:30:53.070
As Thailand will soon launch

00:30:53.070 --> 00:30:55.500
its own national screening mechanisms,

00:30:55.500 --> 00:30:58.320
providing urban refugees
with temporary legal status

00:30:58.320 --> 00:31:01.020
while seeking asylums in Thailand.

00:31:01.020 --> 00:31:03.780
Is also the time to see
whether urban refugees

00:31:03.780 --> 00:31:05.370
will chain their perceptions

00:31:05.370 --> 00:31:07.680
regarding asylum seeking in Bangkok.

00:31:07.680 --> 00:31:11.130
In fact, many do not merely
seek asylums in the cities,

00:31:11.130 --> 00:31:13.590
but have already integrated
into the Thai society

00:31:13.590 --> 00:31:15.060
in some way.

00:31:15.060 --> 00:31:17.100
Whether it is asylums or integrations,

00:31:17.100 --> 00:31:20.157
I believe that urban
power and refugee asylum

00:31:20.157 --> 00:31:22.050
are part of the ingredients

00:31:22.050 --> 00:31:24.360
making the livelihoods
of these urban refugees

00:31:24.360 --> 00:31:28.080
continue to be a path of survival.

00:31:28.080 --> 00:31:30.714
I want to end my talk with
some key implications.

00:31:30.714 --> 00:31:34.140
First, cities may be another factors

00:31:34.140 --> 00:31:36.000
contributing to refugee asylums,

00:31:36.000 --> 00:31:39.150
but it is indeed the essential one.

00:31:39.150 --> 00:31:42.060
Second, urban power
play a significant part

00:31:42.060 --> 00:31:44.190
in empowering refugee agencies

00:31:44.190 --> 00:31:46.473
and also enabling their asylums.

00:31:48.030 --> 00:31:49.680
Empowering refugees therefore,

00:31:49.680 --> 00:31:52.350
require the empowerment of the cities

00:31:52.350 --> 00:31:54.570
and also the urban power.

00:31:54.570 --> 00:31:57.360
And finally, although constructing asylums

00:31:57.360 --> 00:32:00.240
in a non-signatory states
may be challenging,

00:32:00.240 --> 00:32:01.860
the case of urban refugees in Thailand

00:32:01.860 --> 00:32:06.860
that I show in my talk today
indicates that it's possible.

00:32:07.890 --> 00:32:09.750
In fact, the possibility of asylum

00:32:09.750 --> 00:32:13.950
in some non-signatory states
can be far greater than that

00:32:13.950 --> 00:32:16.320
in the signatory states.

00:32:16.320 --> 00:32:17.153
That's the end of my talk.

00:32:17.153 --> 00:32:18.063
Thank you so much.

