that Thailand will use likely
in this December refugee forum

00:39:56.160 --> 00:39:57.720
is that, we already, you know,

00:39:57.720 --> 00:39:59.760
creating the national
screening mechanisms,

00:39:59.760 --> 00:40:02.753
and these mechanisms
could be a replacement

00:40:02.753 --> 00:40:04.950
of the conventions, right?

00:40:04.950 --> 00:40:07.740
So that's the kind of
thing that Thailand feel

00:40:07.740 --> 00:40:10.980
that if the refugee
conventions get updated,

00:40:10.980 --> 00:40:13.050
and then we also have
some other thing else

00:40:13.050 --> 00:40:16.290
that we already doing, so then that...

00:40:16.290 --> 00:40:18.690
Unless that updated version is there,

00:40:18.690 --> 00:40:20.400
then we would sign the conventions.

00:40:20.400 --> 00:40:23.070
Otherwise it would not agree
to sign the conventions

00:40:23.070 --> 00:40:27.519
I believe, and they don't
see the importance of it also

00:40:27.519 --> 00:40:28.740
in terms of that.

00:40:28.740 --> 00:40:30.600
Nevertheless saying that doesn't mean

00:40:30.600 --> 00:40:32.850
that Thailand does not really oblige

00:40:32.850 --> 00:40:35.850
to international you know,
responsibilities at all

00:40:35.850 --> 00:40:38.670
because there are some other instruments

00:40:38.670 --> 00:40:41.610
that make Thailand taking
care of refugees also.

00:40:41.610 --> 00:40:42.690
And what's more important,

00:40:42.690 --> 00:40:44.940
and also is that if you look into the text

00:40:44.940 --> 00:40:47.910
of the national screening
mechanisms, which I mentioned,

00:40:47.910 --> 00:40:50.880
the definitions of our persecutions

00:40:50.880 --> 00:40:54.150
and also the definition of
refugees, it's expanding,

00:40:54.150 --> 00:40:57.450
it goes beyond the definitions
in the conventions.

00:40:57.450 --> 00:41:00.747
So in that case, it's quite
progressive in a sense,

00:41:00.747 --> 00:41:03.533
but then we have to see whether
in the interpretation of it,

00:41:03.533 --> 00:41:06.900
will it retains that
progressiveness or not.

00:41:06.900 --> 00:41:08.790
So that's to answer the questions

00:41:08.790 --> 00:41:11.313
on the design of the conventions.

00:41:12.600 --> 00:41:14.250
The term resilience, yeah,

00:41:14.250 --> 00:41:16.260
I mean I think that's really interesting

00:41:16.260 --> 00:41:19.020
the way you ask the questions
because I have to admit

00:41:19.020 --> 00:41:20.670
that I did not engage much

00:41:20.670 --> 00:41:22.830
with the critical side of that yet,

00:41:22.830 --> 00:41:26.010
and then I try to go along
with the mainstream in itself.

00:41:26.010 --> 00:41:28.563
But when I think about the
resilience here, as I mentioned,

00:41:28.563 --> 00:41:32.624
it's also related to the
capacities of them in itself,

00:41:32.624 --> 00:41:34.980
and it's the lending from the structures

00:41:34.980 --> 00:41:36.690
that resilience emerge, right?

00:41:36.690 --> 00:41:38.340
So I'm not talking about resilience

00:41:38.340 --> 00:41:39.960
of the structure in itself,

00:41:39.960 --> 00:41:43.143
but I'm talking about how the
power within the structure,

00:41:43.143 --> 00:41:46.142
like urban power is the one
that lend this resilience

00:41:46.142 --> 00:41:47.370
to the capacity.

00:41:47.370 --> 00:41:49.200
So at the end of the day

00:41:49.200 --> 00:41:52.290
still focus on the people themselves,

00:41:52.290 --> 00:41:55.500
and how they'd be able
to have that capacities.

00:41:55.500 --> 00:41:57.630
But then when I'm talking
about the capacities here,

00:41:57.630 --> 00:41:59.940
the reason why I decided
to use the term resilience,

00:41:59.940 --> 00:42:02.040
at first when I started off this paper,

00:42:02.040 --> 00:42:03.510
I didn't use the term resilience,

00:42:03.510 --> 00:42:05.580
I using the term capacities.

00:42:05.580 --> 00:42:09.450
But then one of my colleagues
criticize that usage

00:42:09.450 --> 00:42:12.540
in the way that when you
think about capacities alone,

00:42:12.540 --> 00:42:15.300
you are assuming that they just have it,

00:42:15.300 --> 00:42:16.133
but you're not talking

00:42:16.133 --> 00:42:18.690
about how they actually
be able to spring back.

00:42:18.690 --> 00:42:19.653
But in the case of refugees, you know,

00:42:19.653 --> 00:42:21.810
it's not just having capacities,

00:42:21.810 --> 00:42:23.040
but they use these capacities

00:42:23.040 --> 00:42:23.880
to make something else

00:42:23.880 --> 00:42:24.810
happening for them,

00:42:24.810 --> 00:42:27.180
and that means liveing
possibilities and so on.

00:42:27.180 --> 00:42:30.480
So in this case, that's why
I go to the term resilience,

00:42:30.480 --> 00:42:32.790
and I go with the term
resilience, for that reason,

00:42:32.790 --> 00:42:37.320
to also extend the part of capacities

00:42:37.320 --> 00:42:39.570
from not just having capacities,

00:42:39.570 --> 00:42:42.147
but to focus on how
they use the capacities

00:42:42.147 --> 00:42:44.703
to construct the resilience, basically.

00:42:47.670 --> 00:42:52.290
- [Teresa] There is a question
from somebody on Zoom,

00:42:52.290 --> 00:42:53.820
and I'll read that.

00:42:53.820 --> 00:42:54.930
If urban power,

00:42:54.930 --> 00:42:57.840
especially the built environment
and environmental power

00:42:57.840 --> 00:43:00.150
is considered to be a central component

00:43:00.150 --> 00:43:02.730
of refugee resilience in a city

00:43:02.730 --> 00:43:05.670
with such host hostile environment,

00:43:05.670 --> 00:43:09.900
to what extent do development
project like string cleanups

00:43:09.900 --> 00:43:14.730
and other physical changes to
the city affect this power?

00:43:14.730 --> 00:43:18.030
Are there any pathway for
refugees or their advocates

00:43:18.030 --> 00:43:20.703
to be a part of city
planning at this point?

00:43:21.720 --> 00:43:24.720
- Okay, I think this really
depends on the context.

00:43:24.720 --> 00:43:27.960
Now, this is where the
context gonna matters a lot.

00:43:27.960 --> 00:43:29.340
In the case of Bangkok right now,

00:43:29.340 --> 00:43:31.590
I think that refugees are being part

00:43:31.590 --> 00:43:33.115
of the construction of the cities

00:43:33.115 --> 00:43:34.740
because one of the key jobs

00:43:34.740 --> 00:43:36.930
that several refugee groups are engaging,

00:43:36.930 --> 00:43:39.000
is actually the
construction of the cities,

00:43:39.000 --> 00:43:40.380
the construction work and so on.

00:43:40.380 --> 00:43:42.330
And if you look into the survey,

00:43:42.330 --> 00:43:47.330
I mean in other public of
my, on their human capitals,

00:43:47.400 --> 00:43:48.450
it shows that most of them

00:43:48.450 --> 00:43:51.540
identify construction
as being their own skill

00:43:51.540 --> 00:43:52.830
that they already have.

00:43:52.830 --> 00:43:56.550
So in that sense you know,
in terms of being engaged

00:43:56.550 --> 00:43:58.080
in the construction of the cities

00:43:58.080 --> 00:43:58.980
in a way of the create,

00:43:58.980 --> 00:44:01.710
the creation of the build
environment, they were part of it.

00:44:01.710 --> 00:44:03.630
But now in terms of the city planning,

00:44:03.630 --> 00:44:05.100
this is another question,

00:44:05.100 --> 00:44:06.623
that refugees at least in
the context of Thailand,

00:44:06.623 --> 00:44:09.450
they're not able to do so yet.

00:44:09.450 --> 00:44:11.550
And the reason why, because
of course, you know,

00:44:11.550 --> 00:44:13.230
the lack of legal status.

00:44:13.230 --> 00:44:14.520
And because of the lack of legal state,

00:44:14.520 --> 00:44:16.407
it means that they are not,

00:44:16.407 --> 00:44:21.407
they are also afraid of
even going out to be exposed

00:44:21.420 --> 00:44:23.460
or to be visible most of the time.

00:44:23.460 --> 00:44:26.550
And because of the fear of
being visible in that sense,

00:44:26.550 --> 00:44:30.100
it leads them to taking
part in terms of plannings

00:44:30.100 --> 00:44:31.982
or engaging in policies.

00:44:31.982 --> 00:44:35.310
Second thing, not all groups of refugees

00:44:35.310 --> 00:44:37.590
engage or speak Thai language, right?

00:44:37.590 --> 00:44:40.950
Only certain groups of them
that really do speak Thai.

00:44:40.950 --> 00:44:43.710
And because of this, it
also part of the reasons

00:44:43.710 --> 00:44:47.040
why refugees were not you
know, be really engaged

00:44:47.040 --> 00:44:47.873
in that kind of planning and so on.

00:44:47.873 --> 00:44:52.560
And most of them still dream
of transit through Thailand,

00:44:52.560 --> 00:44:56.430
the transit words, the terms
that in the (indistinct)

00:44:56.430 --> 00:45:00.090
it's got constructed to
different factors and so on.

00:45:00.090 --> 00:45:03.240
But also in the imagination
of refugees themselves

00:45:03.240 --> 00:45:04.740
that they want to transit through Thailand

00:45:04.740 --> 00:45:05.820
and go somewhere else.

00:45:05.820 --> 00:45:10.110
And because of the thinking
about transitting to Thailand

00:45:10.110 --> 00:45:11.430
and to go somewhere else,

00:45:11.430 --> 00:45:14.610
it made them less engaged
politically or internal planning

00:45:14.610 --> 00:45:16.320
or the cities and so on and so forth.

00:45:16.320 --> 00:45:18.540
Nevertheless, because of
the resettlement opportunity

00:45:18.540 --> 00:45:22.650
become limited, nowadays
several refugee organizations

00:45:22.650 --> 00:45:25.110
within the countries,
particularly local CSO

00:45:25.110 --> 00:45:26.946
engaged more in empowering refugees

00:45:26.946 --> 00:45:30.690
so that they can be part
of these city planning.

00:45:30.690 --> 00:45:32.670
But I mean, in some other cases,

00:45:32.670 --> 00:45:34.560
perhaps I think it may be viable,

00:45:34.560 --> 00:45:36.750
but in the case of context
of Bangkok at the moment,

00:45:36.750 --> 00:45:39.660
I don't think that it's
the case yet, yeah.

00:45:39.660 --> 00:45:42.240
- [Teresa] And then a follow up from that,

00:45:42.240 --> 00:45:45.575
are children for example,
allowed to go to school?

00:45:45.575 --> 00:45:49.200
So what happens to the
next generation, basically?

00:45:49.200 --> 00:45:50.033
- Right.

00:45:50.033 --> 00:45:52.590
- [Teresa] Do they consider
or view themselves?

00:45:52.590 --> 00:45:54.180
- This is the most important question.

00:45:54.180 --> 00:45:58.170
So, one thing is that refugees
in Thailand can go to school.

00:45:58.170 --> 00:46:00.030
All students can go to Thai school.

00:46:00.030 --> 00:46:03.450
And it's guarantee in the constitutions

00:46:03.450 --> 00:46:06.420
that all kids in the country,
regardless of what race,

00:46:06.420 --> 00:46:08.760
ethnic cities they are,
they can go to school.

00:46:08.760 --> 00:46:09.593
But then when you go to school,

00:46:09.593 --> 00:46:12.030
the school also teach everything

00:46:12.030 --> 00:46:13.410
in the Thai language, right?

00:46:13.410 --> 00:46:14.370
So that's part of the reason

00:46:14.370 --> 00:46:16.350
why some of them do not go to Thai school,

00:46:16.350 --> 00:46:19.260
and then because of some of
them want to just make a transit

00:46:19.260 --> 00:46:21.000
and not make a living in Bangkok.

00:46:21.000 --> 00:46:23.117
So they decided not to send
their kids to the school.

00:46:23.117 --> 00:46:25.620
That's the decision
the refugee themselves.

00:46:25.620 --> 00:46:28.260
Second things was that it's
also about the structures.

00:46:28.260 --> 00:46:30.930
When refugees going to the Thai school,

00:46:30.930 --> 00:46:32.220
what happened is that

00:46:32.220 --> 00:46:35.190
sometimes the principals
may make decisions

00:46:35.190 --> 00:46:37.260
not to admit them into school,

00:46:37.260 --> 00:46:39.150
or even if they're already in a school,

00:46:39.150 --> 00:46:41.970
they not allow or encourage
them to continue their studies

00:46:41.970 --> 00:46:44.460
in a higher level by not
giving them, for example,

00:46:44.460 --> 00:46:45.660
the certificate, right?

00:46:45.660 --> 00:46:46.560
So it's a challenge.

00:46:46.560 --> 00:46:48.690
It's not simply just in terms of you know,

00:46:48.690 --> 00:46:50.250
the access is already there,

00:46:50.250 --> 00:46:53.520
but how the manuring behind it,

00:46:53.520 --> 00:46:55.560
it's become something
that borrowed access.

00:46:55.560 --> 00:46:56.393
And at the same time

00:46:56.393 --> 00:46:58.440
for them to actually climb up the ladders

00:46:58.440 --> 00:47:00.960
and become successful within
the context of Thailand,

00:47:00.960 --> 00:47:04.980
it also limits it because
of that structure in itself.

00:47:04.980 --> 00:47:07.860
Nevertheless, when one
good news that is coming in

00:47:07.860 --> 00:47:08.700
from the case of Thailand

00:47:08.700 --> 00:47:11.310
is that if you look into the convention

00:47:11.310 --> 00:47:13.170
on the Rio of children,

00:47:13.170 --> 00:47:15.600
Thailand is the last country in the world

00:47:15.600 --> 00:47:19.980
to reserve articles of
22nd, which is focused

00:47:19.980 --> 00:47:23.580
specifically on the rise
of the refugee shooting.

00:47:23.580 --> 00:47:25.560
So this year, it's a year

00:47:25.560 --> 00:47:27.630
that Thailand is trying
to you know, withdraw

00:47:27.630 --> 00:47:29.100
from that particular articles,

00:47:29.100 --> 00:47:30.510
and I have opportunities

00:47:30.510 --> 00:47:32.670
to be able to take part
in that process as well.

00:47:32.670 --> 00:47:33.780
So they are laying the power

00:47:35.100 --> 00:47:39.800
to plan for the withdrawal
from the CRC Article 22.

00:47:40.980 --> 00:47:44.067
So that should provide
a pathway for childrens

00:47:44.067 --> 00:47:45.840
and to integrate more into Thailand

00:47:45.840 --> 00:47:47.813
and to get more support for them, yeah.

00:47:51.993 --> 00:47:53.055
Yes, Marcus.

00:47:53.055 --> 00:47:54.780
- [Marcus] Yeah, so I have two questions.

00:47:54.780 --> 00:47:55.890
One is about,

00:47:55.890 --> 00:47:59.700
I was really struck by your
use of like environment,

00:47:59.700 --> 00:48:01.470
particularly like speaking
about the urban environment

00:48:01.470 --> 00:48:04.570
because like from the perspective,
environmental history,

00:48:04.570 --> 00:48:06.840
it's quite separate from urban history

00:48:06.840 --> 00:48:11.430
where urban is not considered
as part of the environment.

00:48:11.430 --> 00:48:14.880
So I'm just wondering like
how does nature figure

00:48:14.880 --> 00:48:16.590
in the kind of research that you do,

00:48:16.590 --> 00:48:20.818
especially thinking about
questions of refugee narratives

00:48:20.818 --> 00:48:25.818
of landscape, memory and
place, like place making.

00:48:26.900 --> 00:48:28.320
Because they are the space,

00:48:28.320 --> 00:48:30.540
they probably come from a
kind of different landscape

00:48:30.540 --> 00:48:32.400
than the bank of urban environment.

00:48:32.400 --> 00:48:34.740
Like how does that change their experience

00:48:34.740 --> 00:48:37.161
or how do they negotiate with that?

00:48:37.161 --> 00:48:39.537
The second question that I have is,

00:48:40.790 --> 00:48:42.930
how does this research

00:48:42.930 --> 00:48:46.860
like relate to like current
studies of globalization

00:48:46.860 --> 00:48:47.730
and refugees?

00:48:47.730 --> 00:48:49.593
I'm thinking specifically about

00:48:49.593 --> 00:48:51.466
like the work of Aron Matthews

00:48:51.466 --> 00:48:56.010
and about like (indistinct) mentions

00:48:56.010 --> 00:48:59.100
the ghetto of the end of the
world, the center of the world,

00:48:59.100 --> 00:49:01.920
where you know, that in
that particular example,

00:49:01.920 --> 00:49:04.560
Hong Kong is both aspirational

00:49:04.560 --> 00:49:08.550
for the kind of middle class
in developing countries,

00:49:08.550 --> 00:49:12.930
but also they're marginalized, right?

00:49:12.930 --> 00:49:14.010
Within Hong Kong society.

00:49:14.010 --> 00:49:16.680
So is there a kind of similar dynamic

00:49:16.680 --> 00:49:20.790
that's played out in your
research that you observe?

00:49:20.790 --> 00:49:21.990
- All right, thank you so much Marcus.

00:49:21.990 --> 00:49:24.540
So let me start from the first questions.

00:49:24.540 --> 00:49:27.390
To think about the
change of the experience.

00:49:27.390 --> 00:49:29.370
Actually, one thing that
I find is so interesting

00:49:29.370 --> 00:49:32.130
was that because of, as I
mentioned in the stories,

00:49:32.130 --> 00:49:34.290
at the beginning of the
Hmong community, right?

00:49:34.290 --> 00:49:37.020
Once they move to Thailand
and the lack of legal status,

00:49:37.020 --> 00:49:39.180
lack of access to justice, for example,

00:49:39.180 --> 00:49:40.710
what would be the alternative?

00:49:40.710 --> 00:49:43.023
Then that is the time
when they adopt, you know,

00:49:43.023 --> 00:49:46.320
like the structure, social
structures that they had already

00:49:46.320 --> 00:49:49.260
in their home countries or into Thailand.

00:49:49.260 --> 00:49:52.589
So it's kind of the
reputations of that structure

00:49:52.589 --> 00:49:54.420
is that already at play there.

00:49:54.420 --> 00:49:57.450
But at the same time,
they also try to match it

00:49:57.450 --> 00:49:59.970
with the context that
is already different.

00:49:59.970 --> 00:50:01.800
Okay, that's the second part

00:50:01.800 --> 00:50:03.478
of the first questions that I answer.

00:50:03.478 --> 00:50:06.240
The thing that I think about
environment, I think that

00:50:06.240 --> 00:50:07.530
you know, when you
think about environment,

00:50:07.530 --> 00:50:10.800
there are multiple dimensions
to environment, right?

00:50:10.800 --> 00:50:13.830
When we hear about the word environment,

00:50:13.830 --> 00:50:17.271
of course, we think about
a forest, nature and so on.

00:50:17.271 --> 00:50:20.100
But if you think about
the second dimensions

00:50:20.100 --> 00:50:22.470
or the second phase of the environment,

00:50:22.470 --> 00:50:24.300
it's something around us here.

00:50:24.300 --> 00:50:26.182
It could be rhetorical in a sense, right?

00:50:26.182 --> 00:50:27.900
Or if we think about
the third environment,

00:50:27.900 --> 00:50:30.630
it could be something that
got described somewhere

00:50:30.630 --> 00:50:31.463
and so on.

00:50:31.463 --> 00:50:34.500
So there are multiple ways
or multiple dimensions

00:50:34.500 --> 00:50:36.120
to the terms environment itself.

00:50:36.120 --> 00:50:38.819
And the way I use it's to focus it more

00:50:38.819 --> 00:50:41.280
based on not just the nature,

00:50:41.280 --> 00:50:43.020
but the amending of the nature

00:50:43.020 --> 00:50:45.240
in the way that allows everything else

00:50:45.240 --> 00:50:46.500
to be built on that nature.

00:50:46.500 --> 00:50:49.470
So nature may be there as a
ground and so on and so forth,

00:50:49.470 --> 00:50:51.000
but on on top of that,

00:50:51.000 --> 00:50:52.650
it got those built environment

00:50:52.650 --> 00:50:54.891
that become part of my explanations.

00:50:54.891 --> 00:50:59.220
So I hope that I answer
most of the first question,

00:50:59.220 --> 00:51:00.087
both parts of the first question.

00:51:00.087 --> 00:51:03.090
The second question is
internal marginalization, yes.

00:51:03.090 --> 00:51:04.050
The answer is yes,

00:51:04.050 --> 00:51:06.316
that they got marginalized
within the cities

00:51:06.316 --> 00:51:10.650
and most of the time many of
them will push into poverty.

00:51:10.650 --> 00:51:12.414
So if you see, I did not show you the data

00:51:12.414 --> 00:51:14.700
that I gathered from the interview.

00:51:14.700 --> 00:51:17.550
The 140 interviews that I did.

00:51:17.550 --> 00:51:21.420
It was the case that most
of the refugees themselves

00:51:21.420 --> 00:51:23.760
after COVID 19 hits really hard,

00:51:23.760 --> 00:51:25.560
they will push out of the jobs

00:51:25.560 --> 00:51:27.300
so they no longer have it at all.

00:51:27.300 --> 00:51:30.825
Like even some of the home
base and workshops so on.

00:51:30.825 --> 00:51:35.133
And they become more
dependent than ever on it.

00:51:37.740 --> 00:51:40.980
Nevertheless, it's not all
the communities are the same.

00:51:40.980 --> 00:51:42.960
Some communities that
have a strong structure

00:51:42.960 --> 00:51:46.537
that they imported from the home countries

00:51:46.537 --> 00:51:48.060
for example, in the Hmong community,

00:51:48.060 --> 00:51:51.120
their resilience was still
going strong and very well,

00:51:51.120 --> 00:51:53.280
they can spring back
pretty quickly, right?

00:51:53.280 --> 00:51:56.970
So in that case, I think the
degree to marginalizations,

00:51:56.970 --> 00:51:58.671
it depends on the degrees

00:51:58.671 --> 00:52:02.700
in which they integrate into the societies

00:52:02.700 --> 00:52:04.350
and how they expand the network

00:52:04.350 --> 00:52:06.330
based on that integrations.

00:52:06.330 --> 00:52:08.603
So it's come down to that questions.

00:52:08.603 --> 00:52:10.830
And then I think that
we cannot say that, oh,

00:52:10.830 --> 00:52:13.575
our group of urban
refugees are (indistinct).

00:52:13.575 --> 00:52:15.300
I mean there are groups that
are really, really marginalized

00:52:15.300 --> 00:52:16.290
comparing to the other.

00:52:16.290 --> 00:52:19.493
So it's a matter of degrees,
I think to a large extent.

00:52:21.224 --> 00:52:25.057
- [Teresa] We have now
a question from Ardlin.

00:52:26.730 --> 00:52:28.560
- [Ardlin] Hi, thank you
so much for your talk.

00:52:28.560 --> 00:52:30.071
I wish I was there.

00:52:30.071 --> 00:52:33.090
So just to follow up on this,
I had a couple questions.

00:52:33.090 --> 00:52:36.360
So the first was this question
about integration, right?

00:52:36.360 --> 00:52:38.280
So between Thai and Hmong people.

00:52:38.280 --> 00:52:41.400
I'm wondering how do they
negotiate the identity?

00:52:41.400 --> 00:52:43.650
You mentioned that they were
able to speak for a good Thai,

00:52:43.650 --> 00:52:45.420
Hmong and different languages.

00:52:45.420 --> 00:52:47.190
I'm wondering to what extent

00:52:47.190 --> 00:52:49.020
