00:52:52.380 --> 00:52:54.840
like Thai customs or practices?

00:52:54.840 --> 00:52:57.570
Do they bring in their Hmong
kind of practices as well?

00:52:57.570 --> 00:52:59.310
To what extent do they conceive of nature

00:52:59.310 --> 00:53:02.580
in a way that Thai people
would versus Hmong people?

00:53:02.580 --> 00:53:05.131
Because for example, when I
was talking to Hmong people

00:53:05.131 --> 00:53:06.453
in northern Thailand,

00:53:06.453 --> 00:53:08.850
they had very different
conceptions of like causality

00:53:08.850 --> 00:53:12.725
for like natural disasters
and how to then address that.

00:53:12.725 --> 00:53:14.069
And for example, talking Tanghang

00:53:14.069 --> 00:53:16.560
the different animistic ways

00:53:16.560 --> 00:53:19.290
of dealing with understanding the world.

00:53:19.290 --> 00:53:21.630
I'm just curious to what
extent they integrate

00:53:21.630 --> 00:53:23.970
you know, in their kind of metaphysical

00:53:23.970 --> 00:53:27.090
as well as kind of like
identity practices and language.

00:53:27.090 --> 00:53:29.220
Sorry, the second
question was about power.

00:53:29.220 --> 00:53:31.050
And in Thai studies there's
just kind of like tension

00:53:31.050 --> 00:53:32.700
between (indistinct) and I'm not, right?

00:53:32.700 --> 00:53:34.650
This idea about kind of like moral power

00:53:34.650 --> 00:53:36.540
versus like an authority over something.

00:53:36.540 --> 00:53:38.310
Does that figure into this form of power

00:53:38.310 --> 00:53:41.160
or is it more a different form
of power like (indistinct)

00:53:41.160 --> 00:53:44.880
or a capability that you're
seeing as an empowering term?

00:53:44.880 --> 00:53:46.440
I'm just wondering how this term of power

00:53:46.440 --> 00:53:47.880
relates between the languages.

00:53:47.880 --> 00:53:51.030
I don't speak Hmong, but maybe something

00:53:51.030 --> 00:53:52.860
that they might be thinking about as well.

00:53:52.860 --> 00:53:54.906
And sorry, the last
question is about networks.

00:53:54.906 --> 00:53:57.330
So you mentioned in the
material power section

00:53:57.330 --> 00:53:58.290
and the informal economy,

00:53:58.290 --> 00:54:00.480
that opportunities
depends on network skills

00:54:00.480 --> 00:54:01.313
and connections.

00:54:01.313 --> 00:54:03.120
And I think this is a
very interesting parallel

00:54:03.120 --> 00:54:05.006
with Hindris paper.

00:54:05.006 --> 00:54:07.440
She did a journal article in Quran,

00:54:07.440 --> 00:54:09.960
and she talked about amid
their legal precarity,

00:54:09.960 --> 00:54:12.510
they have the sense of resilience

00:54:12.510 --> 00:54:14.040
through their networks, right?

00:54:14.040 --> 00:54:15.510
So I'm wondering for these Hmong people,

00:54:15.510 --> 00:54:17.130
what are their networks like?

00:54:17.130 --> 00:54:19.200
What is a good network,
what is not a good network?

00:54:19.200 --> 00:54:22.860
How are they bringing in
their social structures or not

00:54:22.860 --> 00:54:24.630
and what that looks like.

00:54:24.630 --> 00:54:25.620
Thank you.

00:54:25.620 --> 00:54:27.360
- All right, thank you so much Al.

00:54:27.360 --> 00:54:28.260
Because of the questions,

00:54:28.260 --> 00:54:30.900
focus more on the Hmong
community and then as my talk,

00:54:30.900 --> 00:54:33.600
like feature both this
particular community,

00:54:33.600 --> 00:54:35.160
both at the beginning and at the end.

00:54:35.160 --> 00:54:37.251
So I will first have to mention this

00:54:37.251 --> 00:54:38.880
about the network itself.

00:54:38.880 --> 00:54:41.550
The network is the most important
parts of their resilience.

00:54:41.550 --> 00:54:42.900
I mean, particularly
for the Hmong community,

00:54:42.900 --> 00:54:44.670
because of these networks, because of it,

00:54:44.670 --> 00:54:48.240
it comes not simply just because
they're already in Bangkok,

00:54:48.240 --> 00:54:51.030
but it started actually
over a long period of time.

00:54:51.030 --> 00:54:53.610
That even before they move into Thailand,

00:54:53.610 --> 00:54:55.560
they already known some of the people,

00:54:55.560 --> 00:54:56.970
you know, within the Hmong community

00:54:56.970 --> 00:54:59.190
and then they already have a separated

00:54:59.190 --> 00:55:00.630
groups of the Hmong communities,

00:55:00.630 --> 00:55:02.580
like across Thailand, Thais, and Vietnam,

00:55:02.580 --> 00:55:04.230
and they know among itself, right?

00:55:04.230 --> 00:55:05.580
So this network was fostered

00:55:05.580 --> 00:55:07.560
not just simply because
of they move into Thailand

00:55:07.560 --> 00:55:09.120
and they found that network,

00:55:09.120 --> 00:55:12.060
but then they known about this
network for quite sometime.

00:55:12.060 --> 00:55:14.315
And perhaps this is part of
the reasons why they integrated

00:55:14.315 --> 00:55:18.210
into Thailand for that reason.

00:55:18.210 --> 00:55:20.277
Also come to Thailand for that reason.

00:55:20.277 --> 00:55:22.513
The second thing that I
really have to mention

00:55:22.513 --> 00:55:24.150
about the network that they have

00:55:24.150 --> 00:55:25.680
is that if you look or you're keen

00:55:25.680 --> 00:55:27.600
into the employment opportunities

00:55:27.600 --> 00:55:29.430
that they be able to gather,

00:55:29.430 --> 00:55:31.080
particularly for the Hmong community,

00:55:31.080 --> 00:55:32.970
it's actually also in the historic areas

00:55:32.970 --> 00:55:34.950
where the Hmong people used to stay,

00:55:34.950 --> 00:55:37.167
including the Lao Hmong
that came into Thailand,

00:55:37.167 --> 00:55:39.630
and for example in areas
near (indistinct) province

00:55:39.630 --> 00:55:40.650
and so on and so forth.

00:55:40.650 --> 00:55:41.730
So that's, for example,

00:55:41.730 --> 00:55:44.100
once they have no job
opportunities in Bangkok,

00:55:44.100 --> 00:55:46.530
some of the people would
go and do those areas

00:55:46.530 --> 00:55:49.503
in order to get the
opportunities and so on so forth.

00:55:50.820 --> 00:55:52.800
In terms of the identity itself,

00:55:52.800 --> 00:55:55.170
I don't think that I can
comment more specifically

00:55:55.170 --> 00:55:57.960
on the language, because one of the thing

00:55:57.960 --> 00:56:02.670
that I have to say is that
for the language part,

00:56:02.670 --> 00:56:04.950
I communicate with the Hmong people

00:56:04.950 --> 00:56:05.940
through either Thai language

00:56:05.940 --> 00:56:07.470
because of the already, as I mentioned,

00:56:07.470 --> 00:56:09.810
integrated in Thailand to a large degree.

00:56:09.810 --> 00:56:11.820
So the way that they express themselves

00:56:11.820 --> 00:56:13.140
or talk to me, right?

00:56:13.140 --> 00:56:16.050
Always in the Thai language itself,

00:56:16.050 --> 00:56:19.297
but behind the scenes whether
or not how they reconcile it

00:56:19.297 --> 00:56:23.520
with the devil language
and devil cultures,

00:56:23.520 --> 00:56:26.160
I cannot mention more
specifically on that.

00:56:26.160 --> 00:56:30.240
And also into the last part
are the questions of power.

00:56:30.240 --> 00:56:32.310
The question of power is
in terms of more of powers

00:56:32.310 --> 00:56:35.280
and so on and so forth that
I already mentioned also.

00:56:35.280 --> 00:56:36.940
I haven't thought about that much yet.

00:56:36.940 --> 00:56:38.640
So perhaps that's the reason

00:56:38.640 --> 00:56:41.190
why I haven't included in
these presentations for today.

00:56:41.190 --> 00:56:42.960
So I cannot comment more specifically

00:56:42.960 --> 00:56:45.360
on that part also for the time being.

00:56:45.360 --> 00:56:47.340
But then that would let
me think more about it

00:56:47.340 --> 00:56:49.530
because that was one idea that came out

00:56:49.530 --> 00:56:51.330
from the notions of moral power

00:56:51.330 --> 00:56:53.220
that even though like when it comes

00:56:53.220 --> 00:56:56.160
to the policy of managing
refugees in Thailand,

00:56:56.160 --> 00:56:58.260
that would be some form
of the moral powers

00:56:58.260 --> 00:57:01.980
that kind of being used,

00:57:01.980 --> 00:57:03.570
in order to actually allow these people

00:57:03.570 --> 00:57:05.490
to stay in the countries.

00:57:05.490 --> 00:57:08.640
And that got mentioned
several times already to me,

00:57:08.640 --> 00:57:10.470
but nevertheless, I
haven't never get a chance

00:57:10.470 --> 00:57:12.540
to really delve into details of those.

00:57:12.540 --> 00:57:15.663
So I cannot comment more
specifically on that, sorry.

00:57:16.740 --> 00:57:18.750
- [Teresa] I would be
interested in asking you

00:57:18.750 --> 00:57:21.530
to pick up on the last
point that you mentioned,

00:57:21.530 --> 00:57:24.984
I guess it was the second to
last about intersectionality

00:57:24.984 --> 00:57:27.630
and the different
identities of these groups,

00:57:27.630 --> 00:57:31.140
both in relation to before
when we met earlier,

00:57:31.140 --> 00:57:35.853
we talked briefly about these
groups span Somali refugees,

00:57:37.050 --> 00:57:38.940
refugees from Pakistan,

00:57:38.940 --> 00:57:41.010
and then refugees from
neighboring countries.

00:57:41.010 --> 00:57:44.017
So the kind of experience
of moving through the city

00:57:44.017 --> 00:57:46.920
for those different groups
must be very different.

00:57:46.920 --> 00:57:50.403
But also maybe you could
speak a bit on gender.

00:57:52.950 --> 00:57:55.500
Are there anything else that
feels significant to you

00:57:55.500 --> 00:57:58.080
in terms of what impacts

00:57:58.080 --> 00:58:00.960
the way that people wield this power

00:58:00.960 --> 00:58:02.628
that you've identified differently?

00:58:02.628 --> 00:58:03.898
- I think that it's really important

00:58:03.898 --> 00:58:05.460
to mention intersectionality.

00:58:05.460 --> 00:58:07.913
I didn't bring it in into
my discussions here at first

00:58:07.913 --> 00:58:10.620
because it's not related
directly to the powers.

00:58:10.620 --> 00:58:12.450
But then of course, I
mean this urban power

00:58:12.450 --> 00:58:15.420
is not shared equally among
different groups of people.

00:58:15.420 --> 00:58:17.973
So different groups of
people draw different,

00:58:19.200 --> 00:58:21.540
I mean the capacity they can
draw from these urban powers

00:58:21.540 --> 00:58:24.090
is kind of diverse and
different, depend on groups.

00:58:24.090 --> 00:58:26.810
For example, with regards
to the Somali, right?

00:58:26.810 --> 00:58:28.080
As as you mentioned,

00:58:28.080 --> 00:58:31.440
Somalis always get spotted
within the Thai societies

00:58:31.440 --> 00:58:34.337
partially because they look different

00:58:34.337 --> 00:58:36.570
in comparing to like Hmong
people or some other groups

00:58:36.570 --> 00:58:38.700
that are from neighboring
countries, right?

00:58:38.700 --> 00:58:40.800
So the (indistinct) for them
to actually integrate it

00:58:40.800 --> 00:58:43.230
into the Thai societies is limited

00:58:43.230 --> 00:58:44.460
comparing to other groups.

00:58:44.460 --> 00:58:46.610
And then whenever they gathered
together in certain groups,

00:58:46.610 --> 00:58:49.380
in certain areas, they
always get spotlight.

00:58:49.380 --> 00:58:51.510
And then that's how the
arrest and detentions

00:58:51.510 --> 00:58:54.690
of these groups have become
something that got more commons.

00:58:54.690 --> 00:58:58.350
And then in many cases,
I mean many incidents,

00:58:58.350 --> 00:59:00.300
of the arrest and detentions occur

00:59:00.300 --> 00:59:02.970
with the groups of Africans refugees.

00:59:02.970 --> 00:59:05.353
It occurred through the fact
that because of the Thai

00:59:05.353 --> 00:59:09.420
immigration bureaus try
to label some operations

00:59:09.420 --> 00:59:12.436
that focus specifically on
smuggling and so on and so forth,

00:59:12.436 --> 00:59:14.400
of these groups of people into Thailand.

00:59:14.400 --> 00:59:15.780
And because of this,

00:59:15.780 --> 00:59:18.180
what happened is that they
become victims of that

00:59:18.180 --> 00:59:20.490
you know, like arrests and
detentions and so on also.

00:59:20.490 --> 00:59:24.393
So in terms of the intersections
of being, you know,

00:59:27.060 --> 00:59:30.360
and then that's just on
ethnic ethnicities partly.

00:59:30.360 --> 00:59:32.490
But now, another thing
that's more important though,

00:59:32.490 --> 00:59:35.760
is that if we think about gender and race

00:59:35.760 --> 00:59:38.100
also together, right?

00:59:38.100 --> 00:59:40.320
What become clear to me also is that,

00:59:40.320 --> 00:59:42.157
it also matters in the way
that how they got treated

00:59:42.157 --> 00:59:43.740
and so on and so forth.

00:59:43.740 --> 00:59:45.480
That for example, in the case of Thailand,

00:59:45.480 --> 00:59:47.490
even though the arrests mostly occur

00:59:47.490 --> 00:59:49.590
when it comes to the male refugees,

00:59:49.590 --> 00:59:51.186
when it comes to female refugees, right?

00:59:51.186 --> 00:59:55.710
The chance or livelihoods of
getting arrested, it's less.

00:59:55.710 --> 00:59:59.280
And when it comes to
other groups, LGBTQI plus,

00:59:59.280 --> 01:00:01.530
their livelihoods in Thailand
is also very difficult.

01:00:01.530 --> 01:00:03.330
Particularly, if they live together

01:00:03.330 --> 01:00:06.360
with people from their own community, why?

01:00:06.360 --> 01:00:09.270
Because most of these people you know,

01:00:09.270 --> 01:00:10.920
they have imported the
same social structure

01:00:10.920 --> 01:00:11.910
from their own countries, right?

01:00:11.910 --> 01:00:14.583
One of the Somali refugees who are LGBTQI

01:00:14.583 --> 01:00:17.940
that I met for example, got
expelled from the community

01:00:17.940 --> 01:00:22.320
and also experienced violence
within Thailand as well.

01:00:22.320 --> 01:00:24.776
But then he cannot access to justice.

01:00:24.776 --> 01:00:28.170
So the only way out for him
to escape from the community

01:00:28.170 --> 01:00:30.270
and go and stay somewhere
else for example, right?

01:00:30.270 --> 01:00:33.660
So in that case, these multiple
identities of the people

01:00:33.660 --> 01:00:35.190
also have an impact

01:00:35.190 --> 01:00:38.550
on how they're gonna be able
to survive within the cities.

01:00:38.550 --> 01:00:41.670
And even though they already survive,

01:00:41.670 --> 01:00:43.860
it doesn't mean that
that's gonna be the case

01:00:43.860 --> 01:00:45.480
in the upcoming years
and so on and so forth.

01:00:45.480 --> 01:00:48.052
So I think to think more
about intersectionality

01:00:48.052 --> 01:00:50.190
in the context of livelihoods,
I think it's important.

01:00:50.190 --> 01:00:53.220
And then I think that I haven't
done that well enough here

01:00:53.220 --> 01:00:54.630
in these presentations today,

01:00:54.630 --> 01:00:56.100
because I think that different groups

01:00:56.100 --> 01:00:58.710
really draw on the powers differently.

01:00:58.710 --> 01:01:01.920
So at the end of the day, the
degree to refugee resilience,

01:01:01.920 --> 01:01:03.990
which is these different capacities,

01:01:03.990 --> 01:01:06.393
also varies in degree as well, yeah.

01:01:14.159 --> 01:01:14.992
- [Teresa] Questions.

01:01:14.992 --> 01:01:17.010
So I think if you join me

01:01:17.010 --> 01:01:21.150
in thanking professor Kaan
Jittiang for visiting us

01:01:21.150 --> 01:01:24.060
and it's a pleasure to
have you here in person.

01:01:24.060 --> 01:01:24.893
- Thank you so much.

01:01:24.893 --> 01:01:26.767
And thank you so much for having me here.

